       iinntt ssttrrvveerrssccmmpp((ccoonnsstt cchhaarr **_s_1,, ccoonnsstt cchhaarr **_s_2));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       For  a dataset like _j_a_n_1, _j_a_n_2, ..., _j_a_n_9, _j_a_n_1_0, ...  sorting it lexi‐
       cographically yields _j_a_n_1, _j_a_n_1_0, ..., _j_a_n_2, ..., _j_a_n_9.   The  task  of
       ssttrrvveerrssccmmpp() is to compare two strings yielding the former order, while
       ssttrrccmmpp(3)  finds  only the lexicographic order.  This function does not
       use the locale category LLCC__CCOOLLLLAATTEE, so is meant mostly  for  situations
       where  the strings are expected to be in ASCII.  This is different from
       the ordering produced by ssoorrtt(1) --VV.

       What this function does is the following.  If both strings  are  equal,
       return  0.   Otherwise,  find  the  position between two bytes with the
       property that before it both strings are equal, while directly after it
       there is a difference.  Find the largest consecutive digit strings con‐
       taining (or starting at, or ending at) this position.  If one  or  both
       of  these is empty, then return what ssttrrccmmpp(3) would have returned (nu‐
       merical ordering  of  byte  values).   Otherwise,  compare  both  digit
       strings numerically, where digit strings with one or more leading zeros
       are  interpreted  as  if they have a decimal point in front (so that in
       particular digit strings with more  leading  zeros  come  before  digit
       strings  with fewer leading zeros).  Thus, the ordering is _0_0_0, _0_0, _0_1,
       _0_1_0, _0_9, _0, _1, _9, _1_0.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The ssttrrvveerrssccmmpp() function returns an integer less than,  equal  to,  or
       greater  than  zero  if  _s_1 is found, respectively, to be earlier than,
       equal to, or later than _s_2.

